## Conversation
User ran `make install` and saw `cat install.sh >install` / `chmod a+x install` instead of an installation, and asked why. After the explanation they asked for a fix, then asked to commit it.

## Actions
Read Makefile; confirmed no `install` target. Ran `make -p -f /dev/null` to show GNU make's built-in `%: %.sh` rule (`cat $< >$@` then `chmod a+x $@`). Confirmed the untracked `install` file was byte-identical to `install.sh`. Deleted `install`, added `install: install-all ## Alias for install-all` to the Makefile and `install` to the orchestration `.PHONY` line. Verified with `make -n install` (runs the five installers then `install-private`) and `make help`. Committed only the Makefile with `git commit -s -S`; the working tree also held unrelated uncommitted planner and herdr changes that were left alone.

