## Dead Ends
Considered disabling built-in rules globally (`MAKEFLAGS += -r` or `.SUFFIXES:`). Not done: the explicit target alone fixes the reported problem, and nothing else at the repo root has a `.sh` twin that a bare `make <name>` could trip on.

## Hints for Future Agents
The `git-autopush-post-commit` hook pushes master to origin immediately on commit (`✅ autosync: pushed master → origin`). Committing on master in this repo is therefore also a push; do not commit here unless the user wants it public.
Any file `foo.sh` at the repo root makes `make foo` silently produce an executable copy named `foo` unless an explicit `foo` target exists.

## Codebase Discoveries
Root `install.sh` exists for GitHub Codespaces, which runs it as the dotfiles entry point; it just calls `make install-all`. README and CLAUDE.md document `make install-all`, not `make install`, so no doc change was needed.

